'''tyrant†''' (tī′rant), ''v.'' [Early mod. E. also ''tyran''; < ''tyrant'', ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To tyrannize over.
{{smaller block|<poem>What glorie or what guerdon hast thou [Love] found
In feeble Ladies ''tyranning'' so sore?</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., IV. vii. 1.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To play the tyrant; tyrannize: sometimes with indefinite ''it.''
{{smaller block|This encouraged the Irish grandees (their O’s and Mac’s) to rant and ''tyrant it'' in their respective seignories.
{{right|''Fuller'', Worthies, Buckinghamshire, I. 203.}}}}

